The mathematics of CORE-MATH, compiled into Limba (doc/ir.md § 15.5): the
functions sin, cos, tan, atan, exp, log, pow, atan2, asin, acos, hypot,
log2, log10, sinh, cosh and tanh of binary64 and of binary32, correctly
rounded, which every engine runs, so that they give the same bits on every
machine.

Version: CORE-MATH, https://gitlab.inria.fr/core-math/core-math, commit
f6c0c3704b636e511e896beb638288218a8d2425 (25 Nov 2026), branch master.
Licence: MIT, in LICENSE (CORE-MATH's own file, unchanged).

The files are CORE-MATH's, unchanged, from these places of the commit:
  src/binary64/<f>/<f>.c and src/binary32/<f>/<f>f.c for the sixteen f;
  src/binary64/log/dint.h, src/binary64/log10/dint.h (two different
  files), src/binary64/pow/pow.h, dint.h, qint.h,
  src/binary64/atan2/tint.h;
  LICENSE.
They keep the directories of the commit (binary64/<f>/, binary32/<f>/).
build.sh compiles them with -ffp-contract=off -fno-fast-math, without
CORE_MATH_SUPPORT_ERRNO (no errno), and with -include
src/common/core_math.h, Limba's own header that gives every cr_ symbol a
limba_cr_ name; Limba calls them through include/limba/math.h. They need
from the C library fma, fmaf, sqrt, sqrtf, roundeven, round, roundf, fmin,
fmax, fminf, fmaxf, nanf and the functions of fenv.h, all exact in IEEE
754 (fma and fmaf must be the correctly rounded ones), and printf and exit
on a path of pow that its authors hold unreachable.

atan2 is not called directly: src/common/math_atan2.c keeps from it the
arguments with x > 0 and a tiny y / x, on some of which this commit
stops the process (exit), and computes them itself.
